wly acquired  spider grip gets stuck in Gwen's hair  causing her to exclaim I don't think you  know what puberty is the scene is  humorous to adults because they know  that obviously puberty has nothing to do  with gaining spider powers and  everything to do with the awkward  teenage Earnest that inspired miles to  try out Uncle Aaron's move in the first  place however for a kid who hasn't gone  through puberty  they might  fully grasped the outlandish nature of  miles assessment if only it truly did  grant you superpowers better than acne  and Bo right you don't have to be  Sherlock Holmes to immediately figure  out that in into the spider-verse  miles uncle aaron was going to play an  important role in the young hero story  fans of the comics knew that he was  actually the Prowler but even if you  didn't know that going in it became  pretty obvious early on that good ol  uncle Aaron had a few tricks up his  sleeve however when you consider that  this is a spider-man movie the term  uncle should be a red flag everyone  knows that it took the death of his  uncle Ben for Peter Parker to realize  that his actions have consequences and  they can't always save everyone  [Music]  yeah  over here Uncle Ben for young miles it's  the death of his uncle Aaron the  parallel between the two characters is a  brilliant facet of the film that is both  mature and thought-provoking you you  were just just keep going  [Music]  kugo eagle-eyed audience members most  likely picked up on a hearty portion of  the various Easter eggs featured in  spider-man into the spider verse in one  of the films many blinking and missing  moments viewers get a peek at miles as  dad's phone contacts although Jefferson  Davis is trying to call his brother  Aaron you can't help but notice another  more prominent name on the list Steve  Ditko Marvel fanboys will recognize that  as a nod to the late comic book artist  who along with Stan Lee co-created  spider-man and Doctor Strange  however the subtle name-dropping doesn't  stop there  in another scene Myles takes out his  phone to make a call and the audience  yet again gets a glimpse at his phone  book the most obvious attention grabbers  are the names B Bendis alluding to Brian  Michael Bendis and Sarah Pacelli this  duo co-wrote Miles Morales first series  2011's Ultimate Comics spider-man you  probably only notice these subtle  details if names like Ditko Bendis and  Pacelli meant something to you and for  many now adult comic readers in the 90s  and early 2000s that was the case when  it's finally his turn to return to his  own universe John mulaney's Peter porker  aka spider-ham gives miles a touching  farewell humorously gifting him a  massive wooden hammer I want you to have  this before making the jump home before  he ascends he sadly declares that's all  folks  prompting Peter B Parker to ask if he  was allowed to say that quote legally  the line is obviously a reference to the  Looney Tunes character Porky Pig  owned by Warner Brothers who you can  assume has a trademark on the infamous  phrase by questioning the legality of  using the line Peter breaks the fourth  wall to call the audience's attention to  the cartoon pig that isn't porky saying  his famous line  it's a meta nod to a competitor's  classic character but it's humor is  probably lost on children since well  they probably aren't all too familiar  with the concept of copyright  infringement into the spider verse  really starts to gain traction when  Peter B Parker enters the fold but  chubbier more depressed spider-man than  the one from miles as universe during  his introductory scene the audience  learns that this more jaded broken-down  version of Peter ended things with the  mary-jane from his universe because well  she wanted kids and it scared me  although it's presented in a humorous  light this arc of Peter learning that he  can in fact become a good parent is  present throughout the movie for those  clever enough to pick up on it it